      "knowledge_point": "Finding an Integrating Factor for an Inexact Equation: Condition on Y Satisfied",
      "problem": "Suppose we would like to find an integrating factor to convert the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y=0,\\,y>0\n$$\n\ninto an exact equation using an integrating factor. Select the correct options in the reasoning below. For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=\\,{{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $y$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)={{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x})=h(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $h(y)$ is independent of $x,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(y)=\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(-\\int h(y)\\,\\text{d}y)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(y)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{y}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)={y}^{3},\\,Q(x,y)=xy.\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({y}^{3})=3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(xy)=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{P}(\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}-\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}) & =\\frac{1}{{y}^{3}}(3{y}^{2}-y) \\\\  & =3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $y$ only, we can conclude that an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(y) & =\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(\\int -(3{y}^{-1}-{y}^{-2})\\,\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(-(3\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+1/y)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}\u2061(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061{y}^{-3})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}\u2061(-1/y) \\\\  & ={y}^{-3}\\,{e}^{-1/y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by multiplying our differential equation by the integrating factor, we have\n\n$$\nI(y)\\cdot ({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=I(y)\\cdot 0\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n{y}^{-3}{e}^{-1/y}({y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+xy\\,\\text{d}y)=0.\n$$",
